# kbkit

A construction kit for **tissue knowledge bases** — the structured, provenance-carrying
knowledge layer that a single-cell annotation system reads from.

The kit ships **structure, discipline and machine gates**. It does not ship knowledge:
marker panels, composition baselines and disease context still have to be read out of
the literature for your tissue. What it removes is the design work and the recurring
mistakes, not the reading.

```
kbkit init <tissue> <dir>     scaffold a plugin from the schema
kbkit validate <dir>          run every machine gate
kbkit serve <dir>             thin stdio query service over a plugin
```

## Install

```bash
pip install .
kbkit --version
```

## Quick start

```bash
kbkit init retina ./retina-kb-plugin
kbkit validate ./retina-kb-plugin        # a fresh scaffold is valid
```

Then fill in the plugin. `kbkit validate` tells you which rule you broke, by rule id.

## Layout

```
kbkit/
  kbkit/
    schema.py      entity definitions + controlled vocabularies + the rules
    validate.py    the gates; one gate per rule
    cli.py         init / validate / serve, skeleton generated from schema.py
  tests/
    test_e2e.py    drives the real CLI end to end
```

A plugin is one directory per tissue:

```
<tissue>-kb-plugin/
  plugin.yaml            tissue / species / stage declaration, version, build lock
  markers/*.json         cell type -> genes, each gene with direction + provenance
  baselines/*.json       composition references (per-donor ranges)
  disease/*.json         disease layer: cell type x state
  ontology/              ontology sub-graph + concept mapping
  registry.yaml          annotated datasets usable as references / evaluation truth
  query_templates.yaml   literature retrieval templates, filed by inclusion reason
  extensions/            anything the core schema cannot express
  VOCABULARY.json        generated by `init` from the schema — do not hand-edit
```

## The rules the gates enforce

| id | rule | why |
|---|---|---|
| R1 | every marker gene carries a **direction** (`+`/`-`) | an up-marker and a down-marker are different evidence; merging them into one unsigned list makes ranking ill-posed and ties arbitrary |
| R2 | every knowledge item carries **provenance** (PMID or dataset accession) | an item that cannot say where it came from is not admissible |
| R3 | `organism_stage` is a first-class axis | fetal, developing and adult tissue of the same organ are different knowledge domains |
| R4 | compositions are **per-donor ranges**, never point estimates, and need ≥2 donors | two donors are not a proportion |
| R5 | disease context is **cell type x state**, never a new cell type | "proliferating" describes a state, not a lineage |
| R6 | only per-cell author-level annotated datasets may be evaluation ground truth | using our own consensus labels as truth would be circular |

`NO_MATCH` is a legal ontology value. When the ontology genuinely lacks a term, say so;
never attach a near term by hand.

## Three layers, kept separate

* **core** — tissue-independent: schema, evidence discipline, ranking, aggregation,
  evaluation protocol, gates, service shell.
* **plugin** — one directory per tissue, loaded through `plugin.yaml`.
* **data plane** — the corpus, PDFs and raw datasets. **Not shipped**, for licence and
  size reasons. The kit delivers how to build and how to check, never the corpus.

Keeping these separate is the point: if a tissue needs something the core does not
model, it goes in `extensions/` and the core stays tissue-independent.

## Scope

`kbkit` is a research instrument. It produces a knowledge base and the means to check
it; it does not by itself establish annotation accuracy. Accuracy claims rest on an
evaluation protocol run against frozen, independently annotated datasets — a separate
piece of work, and one the gates here deliberately do not shortcut.

## Testing

```bash
python tests/test_e2e.py
```

Drives the real CLI: scaffolds a plugin, asserts each rule is enforced (a gene without
direction is rejected; a zero-width proportion is rejected; a state word used as a cell
type is rejected; …), then serves a query against the plugin and checks the boundary
case returns `NO_MATCH` rather than an invented default.

